c that promotes release of substance P, a neurotransmitter for pain and heat, which initially results in a burning sensation, but eventually leads to substance P depletion and a reduction in pain. It is available in both topical and injectable preparations. Ketamine blocks centrally located N-methyld-aspartate pain receptors, and at low subanesthetic doses can reduce central sensitization caused by surgery and prevent opioid-induced hyperalgesia. At higher doses, ketamine is associated with hallucinations, dizziness, nausea, and vomiting. Gabapentin and pregabalin are nonnarcotics that prevent the release of excitatory neurotransmitters that relay pain signals. They reduce opioid requirements and are effective antihyperalgesic agents (64)."}]}
